        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Gothic</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 300 CE – c. 850 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Family</span><span class="info-stat-val">Germanic, East Germanic branch, beside a few very scantily attested languages; the only East Germanic language with a sizeable text corpus</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Writing</span><span class="info-stat-val">a few runic inscriptions; then the Gothic alphabet, most likely invented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a> in the 4th century for his Bible translation, derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a>, perhaps with some letters of runic or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> origin; its last known use the Mangup graffiti of the mid-9th century</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Earliest records</span><span class="info-stat-val">documents of the 4th century: the Bible translation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a> (311–382), known from mostly 6th-century codices such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-argenteus">Codex Argenteus</a> (188 leaves) and the Codex Ambrosianus, which also holds the Skeireins; possibly a few runic inscriptions, among them the gold ring of Pietroasa</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Status today</span><span class="info-stat-val">extinct, with no modern descendants; spoken at home in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> as late as the 8th century; Crimean Gothic, which survived into the second half of the 18th century, cannot be its lineal descendant</span></div>
