    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Gothic is the earliest Germanic language attested in sizeable texts, almost all of them copies of the Bible translated in the 4th century for the Visigothic Christians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> under their Arian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a>.</li><li>It is closer to Proto-Germanic than any other Germanic language except the scanty Ancient Nordic runic inscriptions, keeping a dual on its verbs, a passive voice and reduplicated past tenses that the others lost.</li><li>It declined from the mid-6th century, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>’ defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and their elimination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> it lost its last role as a church language in 589 and lived on at home only into the 8th century.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>J. R. R. Tolkien wrote a poem in Gothic, “Bagme Bloma”, ‘Flower of the Trees’, published privately in 1936, and in a letter of 1965 he rendered his own name in Gothic as Ruginwaldus Dwalakoneis.</p></aside>
    </section>

