    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">4th century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a>, Arian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the Visigothic Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>, commissions a translation of the Greek Bible into Gothic, in an alphabet most likely of his own making</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">early 6th century</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> lose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occitania">Occitania</a>, where they had used the Gothic Bible</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">6th century</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-argenteus">Codex Argenteus</a>, the best-preserved Gothic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, is copied and kept by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">mid-6th century</span><span class="rf-text">Gothic is in decline after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>’ defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and their elimination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">589</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> convert from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a>, and Gothic loses its last role as a church language in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">8th century</span><span class="rf-text">Gothic is still spoken at home in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, its last attested use there</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">840–842</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walafrid-strabo">Walafrid Strabo</a> reports that peoples around Tomis still speak the language of the Gothic Bible and use it in their liturgy</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">mid-9th century</span><span class="rf-text">Graffiti in the Gothic alphabet are written in the basilica of Mangup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, perhaps its youngest attestation</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/goths"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/goths.jpg" alt="Goths" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ostgoten-fibel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gothic was the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>. The standard theory of the Germanic languages places it in the East Germanic branch, beside a few very scantily attested languages, apart from North Germanic (Old Norse and its descendants) and West Germanic (Old English, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a> and the rest). Some scholars join the other two as Northwest Germanic, on the hypothesis that Gothic was the first to branch off. A minority, the Gotho-Nordic hypothesis, groups East and North Germanic, partly because the 6th-century writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> ascribed a Scandinavian origin to the Goths. The Polish linguist Witold Mańczak argued instead that Gothic is closest to Upper German, and that the Goths’ homeland lay in the south of the Germanic lands, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><p>A few runic inscriptions found across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> are known or suspected to be Gothic, among them the gold ring of Pietroasa, though some scholars doubt that any of them are. The oldest documents date from the fourth century and come from the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a>, or Wulfila (311–382), the Arian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of a community of Visigothic Christians in the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. He commissioned a translation of the Greek Bible into Gothic, apparently made in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> by several scholars in close contact with Greek Christian culture, and most likely invented an alphabet for it. Because the Goths were converted early, runic writing was soon replaced by this new script.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="structure">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulfilas.jpg" alt="Ulfilas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Germania,_1882_1020017_(4358323359)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fondo Antiguo de la Biblioteca de la Universidad de Sevilla from Sevilla, España, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Gothic alphabet was most likely invented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a> himself. Some scholars, such as Braune, derive it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a> alone; others see some letters of runic or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> origin. It wrote long i as ei, after Greek usage, and had single letters for sounds such as ƕ, a voiceless w, as in ƕan ‘when’, and q, a kw sound, as in qiman ‘to come’. Scholars transliterate it into Latin letters, often adding accents and macrons to show how a vowel sounded or where it came from.</p><p>The grammar keeps much of its Indo-European inheritance. Nouns and adjectives had four cases (nominative, accusative, genitive and dative) with traces of a vocative, three genders and two numbers, in many declensions by the form of the stem. Pronouns and verbs kept a dual, a form for exactly two: wit meant ‘the two of us’, weis ‘we’ of more than two. Verbs had an active and a medial voice, a present and a preterite, and three moods; weak verbs formed the past with -da or -ta, strong ones by changing the root vowel, as in giban ‘to give’ and gaf, or by reduplication, repeating the first consonant of the root, as in saian ‘to sow’ and saiso.</p><p>Gothic also lacks changes found in all later Germanic languages: there is no umlaut, so fotus ‘foot’ has the plural fotjus where English has feet, and the old z did not become r. Small particles such as -u, which marks a question, stood second in the sentence and could even split a verb from its prefix: ga-u-láubjats, ‘do you both believe…?’.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="speakers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Speakers &amp; Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/codex-argenteus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codex-argenteus.jpg" alt="Codex Argenteus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/codex-argenteus">Codex Argenteus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_argenteus_-_Cover.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Magnus Hjalmarsson, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gothic texts were carried by both great branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>. The Bible was translated among the Visigothic Christians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-argenteus">Codex Argenteus</a> was preserved and passed on by northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The Gothic Bible was apparently used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occitania">Occitania</a> until they lost it at the start of the 6th century, in Visigothic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> until about 700, and perhaps for a time in Italy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> until at least the mid-9th century.</p><p>The language was in decline by the mid-6th century, partly because of the Goths’ military defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, their elimination in Italy, and geographic isolation. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> it lost its last, probably already declining, role as a church language when the Visigoths converted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a> in 589, though it survived as a language of the home in the Iberian Peninsula as late as the eighth century. In 840–842 the Frankish monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walafrid-strabo">Walafrid Strabo</a> wrote of monks who reported that certain peoples of Scythia, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> around Tomis, still spoke the language of the Gothic Bible and used it in their liturgy.</p><p>A language called Crimean Gothic survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> into the second half of the 18th century, but it lacks certain sound changes of Gothic and so cannot descend directly from the language of the Codex Argenteus. Medieval writers often used the word Goths for any Germanic people of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a>, and some even for Slavic speakers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="literature">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Literature &amp; Learning</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Only a few documents in Gothic survive, not enough for a complete reconstruction of the language, and most are translations from Greek. The largest part comes from the Bible of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a>, of which roughly three-quarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> and some fragments of the Old Testament survive, in codices mostly of the 6th century, long after he died. The finest is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-argenteus">Codex Argenteus</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>, 188 leaves holding a large part of the four gospels; its language is full of Greek words and usages, and its syntax often copies the Greek directly. The Codex Ambrosianus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and the Codex Taurinensis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, 193 leaves in five parts, hold passages from the gospels, the Epistles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nehemiah">Nehemiah</a>.</p><p>Smaller pieces add to them: a Gothic-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> leaf of Luke found at Arsinoë in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1907 and destroyed by water damage in 1945, after it had been copied; four leaves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>; a lead plate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> with verses of the gospels; a calendar, glosses, and two deeds on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>. The only lengthy text known to have been composed in Gothic is the Skeireins, a few pages of commentary on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a>.</p><p>New finds still appear. The Gothica Bononiensia, a palimpsest discovered in 2009, holds what seems to be a sermon quoting parts of the Bible not otherwise known in Gothic, such as Psalms and Genesis. Five graffiti discovered in 2015 in the basilica of Mangup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, from the mid-9th century, include a quotation of Psalm 76 and prayers in a language hardly different from Wulfila’s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="change">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Change &amp; Descendants</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gothic is known almost only in one form, the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a>’s translation as copied two centuries later, so little can be said of how it changed over time. Its sounds, though, show change in progress. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writers rendered Germanic au in Gothic names as au until the 4th century and as o later, so that Austrogoti became Ostrogoti, which suggests that the old diphthongs had only just become simple vowels when Gothic spelling was fixed. Gothic used a stress fixed on the first syllable of simple words in place of the free accent of Proto-Indo-European, and shortened or lost vowels in unstressed final syllables. A change found in no other Germanic language turned fl into þl in some words, as in þliuhan ‘flee’.</p><p>For the history of the Germanic languages Gothic is a fixed point. It is significantly closer to Proto-Germanic than any other Germanic language except the scantily attested Ancient Nordic runic inscriptions, and it serves as the primary foundation for reconstructing Proto-Germanic, which departs from Gothic only where other branches clearly show the Gothic form to be secondary. Features it shares with Old Norse, such as Holtzmann’s Law, which gave Gothic triggws ‘faithful’ and Old Norse tryggr beside English true, have been taken as signs of a special link, but most are shared retentions, which do not prove one.</p><p>Gothic left no descendants. It faded after the defeats of the 6th century, its last traces the home speech of 8th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and the liturgy reported around Tomis and written at Mangup in the 9th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gothic words live on in other languages. Reconstructed Proto-Slavic has several apparent borrowings from East Germanic, presumably Gothic, such as *xlěbъ ‘bread’, beside Gothic hlaifs. The Romance languages keep others: Portuguese ganso ‘goose’ from Gothic *gans, luva ‘glove’ from lōfa ‘palm of the hand’, trégua ‘truce’ from triggwa ‘treaty, covenant’ and agasalho ‘warm clothing’ from *gasalja ‘companion’; French broder ‘to embroider’ and gaffe; and Italian bega ‘quarrel’.</p><p>Because its texts are so early, Gothic is of considerable interest in comparative linguistics. The Romantic age also wrote in it: on 10 February 1841 the Bavarian Academy of Sciences and Humanities published a reconstruction in Gothic of the Creed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a>, and Hans Ferdinand Massmann, the first publisher of the Skeireins, wrote an alliterative poem in Gothic for a feast in honour of Thorvaldsen that year, having translated the student song Gaudeamus into it in 1837.</p><p>Writers have kept it alive as a language of play. J. R. R. Tolkien wrote “Bagme Bloma”, ‘Flower of the Trees’, published privately in 1936, and Gothic was the primary inspiration for his invented language Taliska. Thomas le Marchant Douse put part of Pilgrim’s Progress into Gothic in 1886, David Carlton translated Alice in Wonderland in 2015, and in 2026 a Gothic language option was added to Minecraft.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">In the Research Series</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word<span>Essay</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Gothic was the East Germanic language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, known almost entirely from the Bible translation commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a> in the 4th century and copied in the 6th. Closer to Proto-Germanic than any other recorded Germanic language, it declined after the Goths’ defeats in the 6th century, lingered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> into the 8th and in liturgy into the 9th, and left no descendants.</em></p>

